#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-sense — the box's own records and screens read against what is actually there, and only what does not add up.

  cc-sense              the pass. Prints ONLY what contradicts itself; nothing wrong, nothing printed
  cc-sense --all        every finding, not just the first few
  cc-sense --json       the findings as JSON (always all of them)
  cc-sense --tick       the rider's form: run, write the findings file, log one line, print nothing
  cc-sense selfcheck    fixtures in a HOME, a tree and a tmux of its own — no board, config, pane or repo of this box's

SILENCE IS THE NORMAL OUTPUT, and that is the whole design constraint. The box already has watchers that report
what is happening (cc-reconcile the board, cc-audit the gates, cc-secretary a model's read of the panes); another
one that lists twenty things an hour is a digest nobody opens, and the reader then misses the one that mattered.
So this reports ONE CLASS: SOMETHING THE BOX BELIEVES IS IN HAND, AND NOTHING IS ACTUALLY HOLDING IT. Nothing here
is a lint, a policy or an opinion, and no model is asked anything — a finding is two of the box's own facts
disagreeing, settled by a file read or a string match.

WHY THAT CLASS. Every one of these went unnoticed until a person stumbled into it, because nothing FAILED. A
protection list that matched zero files, so a guard spared nothing while reporting success. An ask pointing at a
verification script deleted five days earlier. Documents describing tools the box no longer had. A routing entry
aimed at a project that had moved. A message typed into a session's prompt box and never submitted. An orch whose
parent project had no live session to answer it. Each looked green because the thing doing the looking never
resolved the name, or never asked whether anyone was on the other end.

IT HAS NO UNIT, AND THAT IS THE ARRANGEMENT. A watcher with its own timer is a fourth loop running beside
cc-reconcile, cc-audit and cc-secretary for somebody to read; this rides cc-reconcile's turn instead, from an
ExecStart in that unit, exactly as cc-handoff's --sweep already does one layer up. Two selfcheck cases hold both
halves — no `cc-sense.*` unit exists, and an existing one names it — the way selftest.sh:1040 holds the sweep's.
The owner's switch is therefore a config key and not an `enable: owner` row: `--tick` does nothing at all until
`CC_SENSE=on` is in ~/.cc/config, because cc-reconcile's timer is enabled on every box and a recurring pass over
the whole box, its panes included, is the owner's call. A plain `cc-sense` typed by a person is never gated.

READ-ONLY, AND IT PAGES NOBODY. It opens files, globs paths, runs one grep, lists tmux windows and reads panes and
`ps`. It repairs nothing (a tool that edits config or docs on its own is a different and far more dangerous thing),
it types at nothing, it starts nothing, it reaches no network and it calls no model. `--tick` writes exactly two
files, both under CC_SENSE_STATE. Whether a finding ever reaches a person is the owner's call and is deliberately
not wired here.

THE SIX CHECKS. Each was measured against this box before it was written, and each was silent on it — a check that
fires on a healthy box is not restraint, it is noise with a rationale.

  selector  a config value that names paths, and NONE of them resolve. A list that selects nothing is the exact
            shape of the guard that protected nothing: the key is set, so every reader believes it is configured.
            An unset key is not a finding — off is a choice somebody made. Neither is a value with one live entry
            and three dead ones: something still matches, and guessing which entries were meant is not this
            tool's business. A SLASH IS NOT A PATH, either: see entries().
  unread    a config key that is set and that NO code anywhere reads. This is what a rename leaves behind: the
            owner's value stays in the file, every reader has moved to the new name, and the setting silently
            does nothing for ever. Only keys with a value are asked about; the search covers this tree and the
            projects beside it, because a key set here is often read by one of them.
  command   a living document that tells you to run a `cc-…` this box does not have. RECORDS ARE NOT LIVING
            DOCUMENTS and this is the difference the check turns on: an audit or an incident note dated in its
            filename or its opening lines is a record OF A MOMENT, and naming a tool that has since gone is
            correct there. Measured on this box: over the whole tree the rule leaves the historical docs alone
            and reads the ones that claim to describe the box now.
  route     a routing entry aimed at a project that has no board. A channel or an orch pointing at a name that
            is not a project is mail with no address. A TARGET IS NOT ALWAYS A BARE PROJECT NAME, and reading it
            as one is how this check first fired on a healthy box: the project is the part before the `/`, and
            `box` is the box itself.
  orch      an orch whose channel NOBODY CAN ANSWER. This is the one place liveness is a fair question, and the
            reasons are two other files': an orch's pane is never typed into (cc-slack:3857-3858) and the drive
            loop never restarts one (cc-pulse:815-818), so nothing brings it back and its messages queue under
            an alias no other session drains. A project whose directory has moved keeps its board, so every
            records-only check above still says the name is fine. THREE THINGS ARE NOT THIS. A project merely
            not running: a message starts one. A live parent session: an `@main` there reaches somebody, and
            its window carries the whole target, so a `<repo>/<track>` orch's parent is the window `alpha/fix`.
            And an orch that has just exited, which is every orch eventually — `archived` only goes true when
            cc-slack's janitor sets it, so this waits out cc-slack's own clock and reports only the channel
            that outlived it.
  unsent    a message typed into a session's prompt box and never sent. The input-box grammar is cc-handoff's,
            copied with a case that fails when the original moves on, plus the one shape cc-handoff has no reason
            to know: the words the TUI itself draws in an EMPTY box. A turn in flight, a modal and a person still
            typing are each excluded too. See check_unsent for why no other reader of a pane answers this.

WHAT IT IS NOT ASKED TO SEE, on purpose. Whether a row's work is progressing (cc-reconcile), whether a session is
stuck or holding a dialog (cc-secretary, cc-msg), whether the gates are red (cc-audit), whether a unit is enabled
(cc-units), whether a document's PROSE still matches behaviour — that last one is a model's job and a model is
what this is trying not to be.

Env: CC_SENSE (`on` before `--tick` does anything) · CC_SENSE_ROOT (the tree to read; default the tree this script
is in — and its parent when that tree is `core/` inside an overlay) · CC_SENSE_DEV (where the projects that read
the config live; default $HOME/dev) · CC_SENSE_CONFIG (the config file; default $HOME/.cc/config) · CC_SENSE_STATE
(default $HOME/.cc/state/sense) · CC_SENSE_TMUX (the tmux binary; a suite points it at a stub) · CC_SENSE_DWELL
(seconds a box must sit untouched before its text is unsent; default 600) · CC_SENSE_SHOW (how many findings a
plain run prints before it says how many more; default 5).
"""
import glob
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import time

SHOW = int(os.environ.get("CC_SENSE_SHOW") or 5)
GLOBC = set("*?[")
# What a value must begin with before it is read as a path. A relative name with no `./` is not admitted:
# `Europe/London` and a `<repo>/<track>` target are both legal relative paths and neither is one.
ANCHOR = ("/", "~", "./", "../")
# Directories a search never descends. Two reasons in one list: vendored trees are somebody else's code, and a
# worktree is a second copy of the tree we are already reading — it can only ever add matches we have, slowly.
SKIP_DIRS = {".git", "node_modules", "worktrees", ".venv", "venv", "__pycache__", "dist", "build",
             ".next", ".cache", ".mypy_cache", ".pytest_cache", "vendor"}
DATE = re.compile(r"\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}")
CMD = re.compile(r"\bcc-[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*")
SPAN = re.compile(r"`([^`\n]*)`")
KEYLINE = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:export\s+)?([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)=(.*)$")
UNIT = re.compile(r"\.(service|timer|socket|path)$")
HEAD = 400          # how much of a document is read to decide whether it is a record of a moment
FIXTURE = "_cc"     # a suite names everything one run makes `_cc<suite><pid>`; none of it is a real record
QUOTE = 60          # how much of an unsent message is quoted back; the point is recognising it, not reading it

# ── THE INPUT BOX, COPIED FROM cc-handoff:523-527 AND NOT IMPORTED ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Copied the way cc-steward copies cc-broker's DECISION_MARKS (cc-steward:92-97): a `cc-*` tool stands on its own,
# and a selfcheck case below reads cc-handoff and fails when the grammar there has moved on — so the two readers of
# "what is in the box" cannot drift apart in silence. Every one of these was learnt from a real pane: the `❯` the
# TUI draws since 2.1, the non-breaking space an empty box is padded with, and the words each TUI writes into an
# empty box — the older one's placeholder, and the current one's, which is this file's own and held to nothing.
PROMPT_LINE = re.compile(r"^[^\w>❯]*[>❯](.*)$")
DIALOG_MARKS = ("Do you want to", "No, and tell Claude", "Esc to cancel", "Enter to confirm",
                "Would you like to proceed?")
WORKING = "esc to interrupt"          # the footer for the whole of a turn
PLACEHOLDER = 'Try "'                 # an older TUI's empty box
# …and the CURRENT TUI's, which is not cc-handoff's and is held to nothing: it draws this in the box itself when
# it has no completion to offer, so the box is empty and the words in it are its own. Found on a healthy box by
# this very check (2026-09-07): a session idling at an empty box was named every turn, for as long as it idled,
# over text no person typed — the exact wolf-crying a pass that only speaks up when something is wrong cannot
# afford. Matched WHOLE, not as a prefix, so a message that merely opens with it is still somebody's message.
SUGGESTION = "<no suggestion>"
DWELL = 600         # how long text must sit in a box before it is a finding, in seconds. NOT a tuning knob: below
                    # it, a person part-way through a sentence is a finding, and a check that fires on somebody
                    # typing is the noise this file exists to avoid. Ten minutes of an untouched box is a stall.

# cc-slack's OWN clock for a dead orch, copied and held to it by a selfcheck case the way the input box above is
# held to cc-handoff's. An orch that simply exits leaves `archived` false in orchs.json until cc-slack's janitor
# archives it — so an orch check that only asks "gone and not archived" names the ORDINARY END of an orch's life,
# every turn, for a day, about a state cc-slack heals by itself. One sweep of grace on top of the idle window,
# because at the boundary itself the janitor has not had its turn yet.
ORCH_IDLE = 24 * 3600     # cc-slack ORCH_IDLE: how long gone before the janitor archives the channel
ORCH_SWEEP = 900          # cc-slack sweep_cycle: how often the janitor gets to look


def now():
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())


# ── the six checks: pure, so the selfcheck drives every branch with no box around it ─────────────────────────

def config_pairs(text):
    """The KEY=value lines of a shell-style config, in order. Comments, blanks and anything that is not an
    assignment are not settings and are dropped here rather than being reasoned about four times below."""
    out = []
    for ln in text.splitlines():
        s = ln.strip()
        if not s or s.startswith("#"):
            continue
        m = KEYLINE.match(s)
        if not m:
            continue
        v = m.group(2).strip()
        if len(v) >= 2 and v[0] == v[-1] and v[0] in "\"'":
            v = v[1:-1]
        out.append((m.group(1), v))
    return out


def entries(value):
    """A config value split into the paths it names, or [] when it does not name paths at all.

    ALL OR NOTHING, deliberately. A value has to be path-shaped THROUGHOUT to be read as a path list: a list of
    repo names, an email list, a number and a URL are all things a reader would otherwise have to guess about,
    and a check that guesses is the noise this file exists to avoid.

    A SLASH IS NOT A PATH. Reading one as a path is how this check fired on a healthy box: a zone name, a
    `<repo>/<track>` target and a `host:owner/repo` remote all carry a slash and none of them is on disk, so the
    check said "there is nothing there" about a key that was working perfectly, once an hour, for ever. A path
    ANCHORS — `/`, `~` or a `./` this run can resolve — and a value that does not anchor is not asked about."""
    if not value or "://" in value:
        return []
    parts = [p for p in re.split(r"[\s,]+", value) if p]
    for p in parts:
        if not p.startswith(ANCHOR):
            return []
    return parts


def check_selector(pairs, count):
    """`count(pattern) -> how many paths it resolves to`. A finding is a whole list resolving to nothing."""
    out = []
    for key, val in pairs:
        es = entries(val)
        if not es:
            continue
        if sum(count(e) for e in es):
            continue
        if len(es) == 1:
            why = f"`{key}` points at `{es[0]}`, and there is nothing there"
        else:
            why = f"`{key}` names {len(es)} paths and not one of them is there: `{es[0]}` …"
        out.append(("selector", key, why + " — whatever reads it is working from an empty list."))
    return out


def check_unread(pairs, seen):
    """`seen` = every identifier some file on the box actually mentions. A key with a value that nobody reads
    is a setting the owner believes is in force. Empty keys are skipped: an unset key does nothing by design."""
    return [("unread", key,
             f"`{key}` is set in the config and no code on the box reads that name — a rename leaves this behind, "
             f"and the value goes on doing nothing.")
            for key, val in pairs if val and key not in seen]


def config_findings(pairs, seen, count):
    """Both config checks, composed — ONE FINDING PER KEY. A key nobody reads whose path is also gone is one
    problem, and the deeper half is that nothing reads it: saying "whatever reads it is working from an empty
    list" about a key with no reader at all is worse than saying nothing. So `unread` is decided first and
    `selector` is not asked about those keys. `seen` is None when the search could not be made, and then the
    unread question is not asked at all."""
    unread = check_unread(pairs, seen) if seen is not None else []
    dead = {s for _c, s, _w in unread}
    return check_selector([(k, v) for k, v in pairs if k not in dead], count) + unread


def is_record(name, head):
    """Is this document a record of a moment rather than a description of the box now? A date in the filename
    or in its opening lines says so, and naming a since-retired tool is CORRECT in one."""
    return bool(DATE.search(name) or DATE.search(head))


def check_command(docs, known):
    """`docs` = {path: text} of the living documents; `known` = the command names this box has. Only names
    inside a code span count: prose says `cc-land-fixes` about a branch, a code span is an instruction to run."""
    hits = {}
    for path, text in sorted(docs.items()):
        for span in SPAN.findall(text):
            for tok in CMD.findall(span):
                if tok not in known:
                    hits.setdefault(tok, []).append(path)
    out = []
    for tok, where in sorted(hits.items()):
        more = f" (and {len(where) - 1} other document{'s' if len(where) > 2 else ''})" if len(where) > 1 else ""
        out.append(("command", tok, f"`{where[0]}`{more} tells you to run `{tok}`, and this box has no such command."))
    return out


def project_of(target):
    """The project a routing target names, or "" when it names no project at all.

    A TARGET IS NOT A PROJECT NAME. cc-slack's valid_target (cc-slack:902-907) accepts three shapes — `<repo>`,
    `<repo>/<track>`, and the literal `box` — and orchs.json and routes.json hold all three: cc-rename's own
    fixture writes `{"C9": "<repo>/t1"}` and cc-sandbox reads a target with `split("/")[0]`. Only the repo half
    is ever a project with a board, and `box` is this machine, which has no board and never will."""
    return "" if target == "box" else target.split("/")[0]


def check_route(boards, orchs, routes):
    """A channel or an orch aimed at a project that has no board. `boards` is the set of project names, and a
    target is resolved to its project first — asking the board about the whole `<repo>/<track>` string reported
    a live channel as dead mail on a healthy box."""
    out = []
    live = {}
    for entry in (orchs or {}).values():
        if entry.get("archived"):
            continue
        target, alias = str(entry.get("target") or ""), str(entry.get("alias") or "")
        if not target:
            continue
        live[f"{target}@{alias}"] = entry
        project = project_of(target)
        if project and project not in boards:
            out.append(("route", entry.get("name") or f"{target}@{alias}",
                        f"the orch `{entry.get('name') or alias}` reports to `{target}`, and no project called "
                        f"`{project}` has a board."))
    for chan, target in sorted((routes or {}).items()):
        target = str(target or "")
        project = project_of(target)
        if not target or not project or target in live or project in boards:
            continue
        out.append(("route", chan,
                    f"`{chan}` routes to `{target}`, and there is no project or orch called `{project}`."))
    return out


def dur(secs):
    """A stretch of time the way somebody says it out loud."""
    m = int(secs // 60)
    return f"{m} min" if m < 90 else f"{m // 60} h" if m < 2880 else f"{m // 1440} days"


def box_text(pane):
    """What is SITTING in that pane's input box: the bottom-most prompt line's content (a taken turn's echo sits
    above it), "" for an empty box, and None when there is no box on the screen at all — a bare shell, or a pane
    that has drawn nothing yet. cc-handoff's box_holds() reads the same line for its own typed string."""
    last = None
    for ln in pane.splitlines():
        m = PROMPT_LINE.match(ln)
        if m:
            last = m.group(1)
    if last is None:
        return None
    last = last.replace("\u00a0", " ").strip()
    return "" if last.startswith(PLACEHOLDER) or last == SUGGESTION else last


def check_unsent(panes, dwell=DWELL):
    """A message typed at a session and never sent. `panes` = [(window, pane text, seconds since it last changed)].

    THIS IS THE OVERSIGHT THE PASS WAS ASKED FOR, and it is the one no other reader of a pane answers: cc-handoff
    knows the grammar but only ever asks whether ITS OWN string is still in the box, and it runs inside a cutover,
    on no timer; cc-msg and cc-slack read a pane to decide whether a key is safe to send, not whether one is owed;
    cc-secretary's rubric tells its model in the same breath not to flag a session idle at a prompt, and its own
    state word for a box with a sentence in it and a box with nothing in it is the same word, `idle`. So the pane
    goes past every one of them and the text sits there.

    THREE GUARDS, and each is a way this would otherwise fire on a working session. A turn in flight owns the
    screen, so the working footer is not asked about. A modal takes the keys, so a dialog is not this — it is
    cc-msg's, and the box behind it is not reachable anyway. And a person mid-sentence is not a stall, so nothing
    is a finding until the window has not changed for `dwell`; typing echoes, so an untouched box is a real one."""
    out = []
    for name, pane, quiet in sorted(panes):
        if not pane.strip() or WORKING in pane or any(d in pane for d in DIALOG_MARKS):
            continue
        text = box_text(pane)
        if not text or quiet < dwell:
            continue
        out.append(("unsent", name,
                    f"`{name}` has a message typed into its prompt box and never sent: \u201c{text[:QUOTE]}\u201d has "
                    f"been sitting there {dur(quiet)}, and nothing is going to take it."))
    return out


def gone_since(entry, t0):
    """How long cc-slack has believed this orch dead, by cc-slack's own clock. `seen` is stamped ONLY while the
    orch is UP — subscribed on that alias, or holding a window whose pane is not a bare shell (cc-slack:4085-4089)
    — and an incoming message never touches it, so it freezes at the moment the orch went and is exactly the age
    the janitor archives on. The fallback chain is the janitor's own (`seen or created or now`, cc-slack:4090): an
    entry carrying neither stamp reads as new and stays silent, because a gap in the records is not evidence that
    anything is wrong, and a value that is not a number is the same kind of gap."""
    try:
        return t0 - float(entry.get("seen") or entry.get("created") or t0)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return 0.0


def check_orch(boards, orchs, live):
    """An orch whose channel nobody can answer. `live(window)` = is an interactive session in that window now.
    Whether a message could START one is deliberately not asked at all, and the second paragraph is why.

    LIVENESS IS NOT A FAIR QUESTION ABOUT A PROJECT, AND IS THE ONLY QUESTION ABOUT AN ORCH. A message for a plain
    `<repo>` whose session is not running starts one (cc-slack's autostart), so "not running" says nothing. An orch
    is the exception, deliberately, in two places at once: cc-slack refuses to type into a pane for an alias
    (cc-slack:3857-3858) and the drive loop never restarts an orch, because a fresh one would mint a new channel
    every two hours (cc-pulse:815-818). Nothing brings a dead orch back on its own.

    STARTING THE PARENT DRAINS NOTHING, so `~/dev/<repo>` being there is no reason to stay quiet. cc-slack files an
    orch's message under the pair (target, alias) and hands a queued entry only to a connection carrying THAT alias
    — "each entry waits for ITS alias (main's backlog is not an orch's)" at cc-slack:3712-3718, the same filter
    delivery itself uses at cc-slack:3826, and the pane door is shut for an alias besides. autostart runs
    `cc <repo>`, which subscribes as main and so takes none of it. An orch whose window is gone while its project
    sits right there is therefore the quiet failure, not the healthy case: every message to that channel queues
    behind an alias nothing is ever going to carry.

    THE PARENT BEING LIVE IS STILL SILENCE, on a narrower ground than “it falls back on it”: an `@main` in an orch's
    own channel routes to the parent session (cc-slack:4147-4148), so while that session runs the channel does have
    somebody who answers when addressed by name. AND THE PARENT'S WINDOW IS NAMED AFTER THE WHOLE TARGET, not the
    project: an orch reporting to `alpha/fix` has its parent in the window `alpha/fix` and there is no window called
    `alpha` at all (cc-slack's window_for at :867 returns the target unchanged, and valid_target at :906 makes
    `<repo>/<track>` orchs a supported shape). Asking about the PROJECT instead read a running parent as
    unanswerable — and it is not the weaker question but a different one: for a plain `<repo>` target the two are
    the same window, and for a track target a live `alpha` is not the parent of an `alpha/fix` channel and must not
    silence it. So the target is asked, and the project only names the board. An orch whose project has no board at
    all is the route check's finding and not this one's, so a moved orch is named once.

    AN ORCH THAT SIMPLY EXITED IS NOT A FINDING YET. `archived` stays false until cc-slack's janitor sets it, so
    "gone and not archived" is the ordinary end of every orch's life for the day that clock runs, and reporting it
    would mean naming a healthy box's normal state every twenty minutes about something cc-slack is already about
    to clean up. So the question this actually asks is the narrow one: has cc-slack's OWN clock run out and the
    channel still not been archived — i.e. is the arrangement that closes these down not closing this one down."""
    out = []
    t0 = time.time()
    for entry in (orchs or {}).values():
        if entry.get("archived"):
            continue
        target, alias = str(entry.get("target") or ""), str(entry.get("alias") or "")
        project = project_of(target)
        if not target or not alias or not project or project not in boards:
            continue
        # "a moved orch whose parent project had no live session to answer it" — the parent is the window named
        # after the WHOLE target, so `alpha/fix` and not `alpha`; the project above only found the board.
        if live(f"{target}@{alias}") or live(target):
            continue
        # …and it has to have been gone longer than cc-slack's own archive clock, or this names the ordinary end
        # of every orch's life for the day that clock runs. Skip while the janitor still has its turn to come.
        if gone_since(entry, t0) <= ORCH_IDLE + ORCH_SWEEP:
            continue
        out.append(("orch", entry.get("name") or f"{target}@{alias}",
                    f"the orch `{entry.get('name') or alias}` has been gone {dur(gone_since(entry, t0))} and "
                    f"nothing restarts one, and its channel was never archived — messages there queue under "
                    f"`@{alias}`, which only that session drains, so they reach nobody."))
    return out


# ── reading the box ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def roots():
    """The tree(s) this box's own documents and code live in. A box may keep the generic tree as `core/` inside
    a private overlay, and the overlay's docs and bin are as much this box's records as core's are."""
    env = os.environ.get("CC_SENSE_ROOT")
    if env:
        return [os.path.abspath(env)]
    r = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)))
    return [os.path.dirname(r), r] if os.path.basename(r) == "core" else [r]


def walk_files(root, want):
    """Every file under `root` that `want(name)` accepts, with SKIP_DIRS pruned."""
    for dp, dn, fn in os.walk(root):
        dn[:] = [d for d in dn if d not in SKIP_DIRS and not d.startswith(FIXTURE)]
        for f in fn:
            if want(f):
                yield os.path.join(dp, f)


def read_docs(trees):
    """The living documents of these trees: {display path: text}, records dropped. Unreadable files are not
    findings — a document this cannot open is one it has learnt nothing about."""
    docs = {}
    base = trees[0]                  # the OUTERMOST tree names every document, so `core/` inside an overlay reads
    for root in trees:               # as `core/docs/…` — and the two walks over the same file land on one key
        if not os.path.isdir(root):
            continue
        for p in walk_files(root, lambda f: f.endswith(".md")):
            try:
                with open(p, errors="replace") as fh:
                    text = fh.read()
            except OSError:
                continue
            if is_record(os.path.basename(p), text[:HEAD]):
                continue
            docs[os.path.relpath(p, base)] = text
    return docs


def known_commands(trees):
    """Every `cc-…` this box can run or start: the executables in each tree's bin dirs, and the unit names —
    a unit is a real target somebody is told to enable, and it has no file in bin/."""
    known = set()
    for root in trees:
        for d in ("bin", "bin-private"):
            p = os.path.join(root, d)
            if os.path.isdir(p):
                known |= {f for f in os.listdir(p) if f.startswith("cc-")}
        p = os.path.join(root, "config", "systemd-user")
        if os.path.isdir(p):
            known |= {UNIT.sub("", f) for f in os.listdir(p) if f.startswith("cc-")}
    return known


def mentioned(keys, trees, dev):
    """Which of `keys` appear anywhere in the code beside us — one grep over the trees and the projects. Returns
    None when the search could not be made: A CHECK THAT COULD NOT MEASURE MUST NOT REPORT. Saying "nothing reads
    this key" because grep was missing or the walk timed out is exactly the false green this file is against."""
    places = [t for t in trees if os.path.isdir(t)]
    if os.path.isdir(dev):
        places.append(dev)
    if not places or not keys:
        return None
    args = ["grep", "-rhoF", "--binary-files=without-match"]
    args += [f"--exclude-dir={d}" for d in sorted(SKIP_DIRS)]
    for k in sorted(keys):
        args += ["-e", k]
    args += ["--"] + places      # NEVER omit these: `grep -r` with no operand searches the CALLER'S directory,
    try:                         # which answered "something reads it" out of whatever tree the run happened in
        p = subprocess.run(args, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=180, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    if p.returncode > 1:                       # 0 = matches, 1 = none, anything else = it did not do the search
        return None
    return set(p.stdout.split())


def boards_of(home):
    """The project names this box has boards for. A suite's fixture board is not a project (cc-board's rule)."""
    d = os.path.join(home, ".cc", "boards")
    return {os.path.basename(p)[:-5] for p in glob.glob(os.path.join(d, "*.json"))
            if not os.path.basename(p).startswith(FIXTURE)}


def load(path):
    """A JSON file, or None. A box without Slack has no routing tables, and that is not a contradiction."""
    try:
        with open(path) as fh:
            v = json.load(fh)
        return v if isinstance(v, dict) else None
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None


def count_paths(entry):
    e = os.path.expanduser(entry)
    if GLOBC & set(entry):
        try:
            return len(glob.glob(e))
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            return 1                            # an unglobbable pattern is not evidence that nothing matches
    return 1 if os.path.exists(e) else 0


def tmux(*args):
    """One tmux command, or None when tmux could not be asked. CC_SENSE_TMUX names the binary so a suite drives
    this over a stub and NEVER over the panes of the box it is running on."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run([os.environ.get("CC_SENSE_TMUX") or "tmux", *args],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    return p.stdout if p.returncode == 0 else None


def sessions():
    """Every window of the shared `main` server with an interactive session in it, as
    [(window name, pane text, seconds since that window last changed)] — or None when tmux could not be asked,
    which is A CHECK THAT COULD NOT MEASURE and reports nothing rather than calling every session gone."""
    out = tmux("list-windows", "-t", "main", "-F",
               "#{window_id}\t#{window_name}\t#{pane_pid}\t#{window_activity}")
    if out is None:
        return None
    kids, comm = proc_tree()
    found, t0 = [], time.time()
    for ln in out.splitlines():
        f = ln.split("\t")
        if len(f) != 4 or f[1].startswith(FIXTURE):
            continue
        if not interactive(f[2], kids, comm):
            continue
        pane = tmux("capture-pane", "-p", "-t", f[0])
        if pane is None:
            continue
        try:
            quiet = max(0.0, t0 - float(f[3]))
        except ValueError:
            continue                    # no activity stamp is no dwell, and dwell is what keeps a typist quiet
        found.append((f[1], pane, quiet))
    return found


def proc_tree():
    """(pid -> child pids, pid -> command name) for the whole box. `comm` and never the argv: a `claude` carries
    its entire prompt there, and this file has no business holding one."""
    kids, comm = {}, {}
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["ps", "-eo", "pid=,ppid=,comm="], capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=60, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return kids, comm
    if p.returncode != 0:
        return kids, comm
    for ln in p.stdout.splitlines():
        f = ln.split(None, 2)
        if len(f) == 3:
            kids.setdefault(f[1], []).append(f[0])
            comm[f[0]] = f[2].strip()
    return kids, comm


def interactive(pane_pid, kids, comm):
    """Is there an interactive `claude` ANYWHERE under that pane's shell? cc-pulse's question and its reasons
    (cc-pulse:247-254): a handoff successor runs `bash -c '… cc __runnext …'`, so its claude is a grandchild and
    the direct-child test calls a live session dead — and a headless `claude -p` has no box to type into."""
    seen, stack = set(), list(kids.get(str(pane_pid), []))
    while stack:
        pid = stack.pop()
        if pid in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(pid)
        if comm.get(pid) == "claude" and not headless(pid):
            return True
        stack.extend(kids.get(pid, []))
    return False


def headless(pid):
    """A `claude -p` prints and exits (cc-pulse:276-283). Read from /proc, so no argv is ever put in a variable."""
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/cmdline", "rb") as fh:
            argv = fh.read().split(b"\0")
    except OSError:
        return False
    return b"-p" in argv or b"--print" in argv


def switched_on(pairs):
    """Is the rider allowed to run at all? `CC_SENSE=on` in the config, and OFF is the shipped state.

    THE SWITCH IS A CONFIG KEY BECAUSE THERE IS NO UNIT TO SWITCH. This has no timer of its own on purpose (see
    the header), so it rides one that every box enables — and a recurring pass over the whole box, its panes
    included, is the owner's call and not the installer's. That is the same call the `enable: owner` row of a unit
    would have carried, kept in the one place left to keep it. A plain `cc-sense` typed by a person is never
    gated: reading the box by hand is the whole point of the command."""
    return dict(pairs).get("CC_SENSE", "").strip().lower() in ("1", "on", "yes", "true")


def config(path):
    """The config's KEY=value pairs. A box with no config file is not a contradiction; it is a box with no keys."""
    try:
        with open(path, errors="replace") as fh:
            return config_pairs(fh.read())
    except OSError:
        return []


def look():
    """The whole pass: every check, over this box. Returns the findings, most certain first."""
    home = os.path.expanduser("~")
    trees = roots()
    cfg = os.environ.get("CC_SENSE_CONFIG") or os.path.join(home, ".cc", "config")
    dev = os.environ.get("CC_SENSE_DEV") or os.path.join(home, "dev")
    pairs = config(cfg)
    out = config_findings(pairs, mentioned({k for k, v in pairs if v}, trees, dev), count_paths)
    out += check_command(read_docs(trees), known_commands(trees))
    slack = os.path.join(home, ".cc", "slack")
    boards, orchs = boards_of(home), load(os.path.join(slack, "orchs.json"))
    out += check_route(boards, orchs, load(os.path.join(slack, "routes.json")))
    # The two the panes and the process table answer. Both are asked ONCE — one `tmux list-windows`, one `ps` —
    # and both go quiet rather than guess when tmux could not be asked at all.
    panes = sessions()
    if panes is not None:
        alive = {name for name, _p, _q in panes}
        out += check_orch(boards, orchs, alive.__contains__)
        out += check_unsent(panes, float(os.environ.get("CC_SENSE_DWELL") or DWELL))
    return out


# ── the mouth ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def report(found, show):
    """One line each, the way a colleague points at one thing. Capped, because a wall of them is the failure
    mode this whole file is arranged against — the rest are one command away."""
    for _check, _subject, why in found[:show]:
        print(why)
    if len(found) > show:
        print(f"({len(found) - show} more — cc-sense --all)")


def tick(found, state):
    """Write what stands, and one log line. The file is REPLACED every run: a finding that cleared is gone from
    it, so a reader never has to work out which half of the file is still true."""
    try:
        os.makedirs(state, exist_ok=True)
        with open(os.path.join(state, "findings.json"), "w") as fh:
            json.dump({"at": now(), "findings": [{"check": c, "subject": s, "why": w} for c, s, w in found]},
                      fh, indent=1)
        with open(os.path.join(state, "sense.log"), "a") as fh:
            fh.write(f"{now()} {len(found)} finding(s)"
                     + ("".join(f" {c}:{s}" for c, s, _w in found) if found else "") + "\n")
    except OSError as e:
        print(f"cc-sense: could not write {state}: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    return 0


def main(argv):
    if argv and argv[0] == "selfcheck":
        return selfcheck()
    show, as_json, quiet = SHOW, False, False
    for a in argv:
        if a == "--all":
            show = 10 ** 6
        elif a == "--json":
            as_json = True
        elif a == "--tick":
            quiet = True
        else:
            print(__doc__.strip().splitlines()[0], file=sys.stderr)
            print("usage: cc-sense [--all] [--json] [--tick] | cc-sense selfcheck", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    if quiet and not switched_on(config(os.environ.get("CC_SENSE_CONFIG")
                                       or os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".cc", "config"))):
        return 0                     # the rider is off: nothing read, nothing written, and the host timer is green
    found = look()
    rc = 0
    if quiet:
        rc = tick(found, os.environ.get("CC_SENSE_STATE")
                  or os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".cc", "state", "sense"))
    elif as_json:
        json.dump([{"check": c, "subject": s, "why": w} for c, s, w in found], sys.stdout, indent=1)
        print()
    else:
        report(found, show)
    return rc          # a finding is this tool working, never a failed run: the timer's unit must stay green


# ── selfcheck ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def handoff_grammar():
    """cc-handoff's input-box grammar, read out of its source. THE COPY ABOVE IS HELD TO THIS. cc-handoff owns the
    box — it types into one and waits for the turn to take it — and this file only reads it; a copy that drifted
    would go on matching nothing and report that every session is fine, which is the exact false green the whole
    file is against. cc-steward holds its copy of cc-broker's decision words the same way (cc-steward:92-97).
    Returns None when the sibling cannot be read, so the case FAILS rather than passing on a comparison it could
    not make."""
    import ast
    src = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "cc-handoff")
    try:
        with open(src, errors="replace") as fh:
            text = fh.read()
    except OSError:
        return None
    want = {}
    for node in ast.parse(text).body:
        if not isinstance(node, ast.Assign) or not isinstance(node.targets[0], ast.Name):
            continue
        name = node.targets[0].id
        try:
            if name == "PROMPT_LINE" and isinstance(node.value, ast.Call) and node.value.args:
                want[name] = ast.literal_eval(node.value.args[0])
            elif name in ("DIALOG_MARKS", "WORKING"):
                want[name] = ast.literal_eval(node.value)
        except ValueError:
            return None
    return (want.get("PROMPT_LINE"), want.get("DIALOG_MARKS"), want.get("WORKING"),
            PLACEHOLDER if PLACEHOLDER in text else "")


def slack_clock():
    """cc-slack's archive clock, read out of its source. ORCH_IDLE ABOVE IS HELD TO THIS, the way the input box is
    held to cc-handoff's. cc-slack decides when a dead orch's channel is archived and this file only waits for that
    to have happened; a copy that drifted low would go back to naming every orch that ever exited, and one that
    drifted high would sit on a channel that really was stranded. Returns None when the sibling cannot be read, so
    the case FAILS rather than passing on a comparison it could not make.

    ONLY THE IDLE WINDOW IS READ BACK. The janitor's cadence is `n % 30` of a 30 s tick (cc-slack:5889), two
    numbers that mean 15 min only when multiplied, so ORCH_SWEEP is a grace figure and not a copy — being a
    little generous with it costs one silent turn, and that is the direction this file errs in anyway."""
    src = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "cc-slack")
    try:
        with open(src, errors="replace") as fh:
            text = fh.read()
    except OSError:
        return None
    m = re.search(r"^ORCH_IDLE\s*=\s*(\d+)\s*\*\s*(\d+)\b", text, re.M)
    return int(m.group(1)) * int(m.group(2)) if m else None


def selfcheck():
    """Every check driven BOTH WAYS — a fixture where it fires and a fixture where it stays silent — plus one
    end-to-end run in a HOME and a tree of its own. No board, no config, no document and no project of this
    box's is read, and nothing outside the temp dir is written. Usage: cc-sense selfcheck"""
    import shutil
    import tempfile
    p = f = 0

    def ok(name, cond):
        nonlocal p, f
        if cond:
            p += 1
        else:
            f += 1
            print(f"  ✗ {name}")

    def subjects(found):
        return [s for _c, s, _w in found]

    # ── selector ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    have = {"/there", "/also"}
    cnt = lambda e: (3 if e == "/glob/*" else 0) if GLOBC & set(e) else (1 if e in have else 0)
    ok("selector: a key whose only path is gone fires",
       subjects(check_selector([("K", "/gone")], cnt)) == ["K"])
    ok("selector: a key whose path is there is silent",
       check_selector([("K", "/there")], cnt) == [])
    ok("selector: a list where nothing resolves fires, and says how many it named",
       [(s, "names 2 paths" in w) for _c, s, w in check_selector([("K", "/gone /missing")], cnt)] == [("K", True)])
    ok("selector: a list where ONE entry resolves is silent — something still matches, and guessing which "
       "entry was meant is not this tool's business",
       check_selector([("K", "/gone /there")], cnt) == [])
    ok("selector: a glob that matches something is silent, one that matches nothing fires",
       check_selector([("A", "/glob/*")], cnt) == [] and subjects(check_selector([("B", "/no/*")], cnt)) == ["B"])
    ok("selector: an unset key is never a finding — off is a choice somebody made",
       check_selector([("K", "")], cnt) == [])
    ok("selector: a value that is not path-shaped throughout is not read as paths (repo lists, emails, numbers, "
       "flags, URLs)",
       check_selector([("A", "repo1, repo2"), ("B", "a@b.com, c@d.com"), ("C", "5201"),
                       ("D", "--no-net /gone"), ("E", "https://example.invalid/x")], cnt) == [])
    ok("selector: A SLASH IS NOT A PATH — a zone name, a `<repo>/<track>` target and a `host:owner/repo` remote "
       "all carry one and none is on disk, and reading them as paths fired this check on a healthy box on every run",
       check_selector([("CC_TZ", "Europe/London"), ("T", "repo/track"),
                       ("R", "git@example.invalid:owner/repo.git")], lambda e: 0) == [])
    ok("selector: a value that ANCHORS is still read as a path — `/`, `~` and `./` each, so the fix that silenced "
       "the slash did not silence the check itself",
       subjects(check_selector([("A", "/gone"), ("B", "~/gone"), ("C", "./gone")], lambda e: 0)) == ["A", "B", "C"])
    ok("selector: the entry it quotes is the first one it was given, verbatim",
       check_selector([("K", "~/nope/*.json")], lambda e: 0)[0][2].count("`~/nope/*.json`") == 1)

    # ── unread ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ok("unread: a set key nobody mentions fires",
       subjects(check_unread([("OLD_NAME", "x")], {"NEW_NAME"})) == ["OLD_NAME"])
    ok("unread: a set key something reads is silent",
       check_unread([("NEW_NAME", "x")], {"NEW_NAME"}) == [])
    ok("unread: an EMPTY key nobody reads is silent — it is doing nothing either way",
       check_unread([("OLD_NAME", "")], {"NEW_NAME"}) == [])
    ok("unread: the check is skipped entirely when the search could not be made, rather than reporting every "
       "key as unread — that false green is the shape this whole tool is against",
       mentioned({"K"}, ["/no/such/tree/at/all"], "/no/such/dev") is None)
    C = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cc-sense-cwd-")
    try:
        os.makedirs(f"{C}/here")
        os.makedirs(f"{C}/there")
        with open(f"{C}/here/reader", "w") as fh:
            fh.write("KEY_ONLY_HERE\n")
        cwd0 = os.getcwd()
        os.chdir(f"{C}/here")
        try:
            ok("unread: the search reads the places it was GIVEN and never the caller's directory — `grep -r` "
               "with no path operand falls back to `.`, which answered 'something reads this key' out of "
               "whatever tree the run happened to start in",
               mentioned({"KEY_ONLY_HERE"}, [f"{C}/there"], f"{C}/nodev") == set())
        finally:
            os.chdir(cwd0)
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(C, ignore_errors=True)

    # ── the two config checks composed: one finding per key ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ok("config: a key NOTHING reads whose path is also gone is ONE finding, the deeper one — telling somebody "
       "'whatever reads it is working from an empty list' about a key with no reader at all is worse than silence",
       [(c, s) for c, s, _w in config_findings([("K", "/gone")], set(), cnt)] == [("unread", "K")])
    ok("config: a key something DOES read, whose path is gone, is the selector finding",
       [(c, s) for c, s, _w in config_findings([("K", "/gone")], {"K"}, cnt)] == [("selector", "K")])
    ok("config: when the search could not be made, the unread question is not asked and the selector one still is",
       [(c, s) for c, s, _w in config_findings([("K", "/gone")], None, cnt)] == [("selector", "K")])

    # ── command ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    known = {"cc-land", "cc-slackd"}
    ok("command: a living doc telling you to run a command the box lacks fires",
       subjects(check_command({"d.md": "run `cc-gone hello`"}, known)) == ["cc-gone"])
    ok("command: a command the box has is silent",
       check_command({"d.md": "run `cc-land queue`"}, known) == [])
    ok("command: a unit name counts as a command the box has — it is a real thing to enable, with no file in bin/",
       check_command({"d.md": "`cc-slackd` runs the daemon"}, known) == [])
    ok("command: the name has to be in a code span — prose naming a branch or a track is not an instruction",
       check_command({"d.md": "the cc-land-fixes track landed"}, known) == [])
    ok("command: one finding per missing command, naming a document and counting the rest",
       [(s, "(and 2 other documents)" in w) for _c, s, w in
        check_command({"a.md": "`cc-gone`", "b.md": "`cc-gone`", "c.md": "`cc-gone`"}, known)]
       == [("cc-gone", True)])
    ok("record: a document dated in its filename is a record of a moment, not a description of the box now",
       is_record("2026-08-27-audit.md", "# Audit") and not is_record("DESIGN.md", "# Design\n\nLayered:"))
    ok("record: …and so is one dated in its opening lines, which is how a running audit log reads",
       is_record("AUDIT.md", "# Audit (2026-08-26, after the autonomous build)\n"))
    N = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cc-sense-nested-")
    try:
        os.makedirs(f"{N}/core/docs")
        os.makedirs(f"{N}/docs")
        os.makedirs(f"{N}/.claude/worktrees/copy/docs")
        for at, t in ((f"{N}/core/docs/DESIGN.md", "# Design\n"), (f"{N}/docs/BOX.md", "# Box\n"),
                      (f"{N}/.claude/worktrees/copy/docs/DESIGN.md", "# Design\n")):
            with open(at, "w") as fh:
                fh.write(t)
        ok("docs: a tree kept as `core/` inside an overlay is named from the OUTERMOST tree, so the file the two "
           "walks both reach lands on one name (`core/docs/DESIGN.md`) instead of appearing twice",
           sorted(read_docs([N, f"{N}/core"])) == ["core/docs/DESIGN.md", "docs/BOX.md"])
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(N, ignore_errors=True)

    # ── route ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    boards = {"alpha", "beta"}
    orchs = {"C1": {"target": "alpha", "alias": "site", "name": "alpha-site-x1"},
             "C2": {"target": "gone", "alias": "old", "name": "gone-old-x2"},
             "C3": {"target": "vanished", "alias": "a", "name": "arch", "archived": True}}
    ok("route: an orch reporting to a project with no board fires",
       subjects(check_route(boards, orchs, {})) == ["gone-old-x2"])
    ok("route: an orch whose project has a board is silent, and an ARCHIVED one is never asked about",
       subjects(check_route(boards, {"C1": orchs["C1"], "C3": orchs["C3"]}, {})) == [])
    ok("route: a channel routed to a name that is neither a project nor an orch fires",
       subjects(check_route(boards, orchs, {"#x": "nowhere"})) == ["gone-old-x2", "#x"])
    ok("route: a channel routed to a project, or to a live orch's own address, is silent",
       check_route(boards, {"C1": orchs["C1"]}, {"#a": "beta", "#b": "alpha@site"}) == [])
    ok("route: no routing tables at all is silent — a box without Slack is not a contradiction",
       check_route(boards, None, None) == [])
    # …and the three shapes a target comes in. Read whole, `<repo>/<track>` and `box` are both dead mail on a
    # healthy box, which is a check firing on a working thing — the worst thing this tool can do.
    ok("route: a `<repo>/<track>` target is resolved to its project, so a live project's track is silent",
       check_route(boards, {"C4": {"target": "alpha/fix", "alias": "t", "name": "alpha-fix"}},
                   {"#beta--t1": "beta/t1"}) == [])
    ok("route: a `<repo>/<track>` whose REPO has no board still fires — resolving the name is not excusing it",
       subjects(check_route(boards, {"C5": {"target": "gone/fix", "alias": "t", "name": "gone-fix"}},
                            {"#g": "gone/t1"})) == ["gone-fix", "#g"])
    ok("route: the literal target `box` is this machine, which has no board and never will — never a finding",
       check_route(boards, {"C6": {"target": "box", "alias": "b", "name": "box-b"}}, {"#box": "box"}) == [])

    # ── unsent: a message typed at a session and never sent ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    def pane(box, foot="  ? for shortcuts"):
        return f"a line of output\n──────────\n❯ {box}\n──────────\n{foot}\n"

    ok("unsent: text sitting in an idle box past the dwell fires, and quotes what was typed",
       [(s, "can you rebase" in w) for _c, s, w in
        check_unsent([("alpha", pane("can you rebase that"), 3600)], 600)] == [("alpha", True)])
    ok("unsent: an EMPTY box is silent — that is every idle session on the box",
       check_unsent([("alpha", pane(""), 3600)], 600) == [])
    ok("unsent: a box still being typed in is silent — the dwell is what separates a person mid-sentence from a "
       "message nothing is going to take",
       check_unsent([("alpha", pane("half a question typed and not yet sen"), 30)], 600) == [])
    ok("unsent: a session MID-TURN is silent even with the box full — the turn owns the screen and will take it",
       check_unsent([("alpha", pane("queued up behind the turn", "  esc to interrupt"), 3600)], 600) == [])
    ok("unsent: a pane holding a DIALOG is silent — the keys go into the modal, and the modal is cc-msg's",
       check_unsent([("alpha", pane("typed", "Do you want to proceed?"), 3600)], 600) == []
       and check_unsent([("alpha", pane("typed", "Would you like to proceed?"), 3600)], 600) == [])
    ok("unsent: a bare shell and a pane that has drawn nothing are both silent — no box, nothing to be unsent in",
       check_unsent([("sh", "user@host:~$ ls\ncore  docs\n", 3600), ("blank", "   \n\n", 3600)], 600) == [])
    ok("unsent: the three shapes an EMPTY box really has — the non-breaking space the TUI pads it with, the older "
       "placeholder, and the current one's `<no suggestion>` — are all empty, not text somebody typed",
       box_text("❯ \u00a0") == "" and box_text('❯ Try "how does this work?"') == ""
       and box_text("❯ \u00a0<no suggestion>") == ""
       and box_text("❯ real text") == "real text" and box_text("no box here") is None)
    ok("unsent: a session idling at `<no suggestion>` is silent, and this is the whole check being worth reading — "
       "the TUI writes those words in an EMPTY box when it has nothing to offer, and naming them would report a "
       "session that is simply waiting, every turn, for as long as it waits. Found live on this box by this check "
       "(2026-09-07). Matched whole, so a message that merely opens with the words is still a message",
       check_unsent([("alpha", pane("\u00a0<no suggestion>"), 3600)], 600) == []
       and [s for _c, s, _w in check_unsent([("alpha", pane("<no suggestion> is what it gave me"), 3600)], 600)]
       == ["alpha"])
    ok("unsent: the box is the BOTTOM-MOST prompt line — a taken turn's echo sits above it and is not unsent",
       box_text("❯ what I asked before\n  …the answer…\n❯ \u00a0") == "")
    ok("unsent: the grammar is cc-handoff's, letter for letter — it owns the input box, this only reads it, and a "
       "copy that drifted would quietly stop seeing the thing it was written for",
       handoff_grammar() == (PROMPT_LINE.pattern, DIALOG_MARKS, WORKING, PLACEHOLDER))

    # ── orch: a channel nobody can answer ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ob = {"alpha", "beta"}
    # cc-slack has had its turn to archive this one and has not, which is the whole question the check asks.
    dead = time.time() - ORCH_IDLE - ORCH_SWEEP - 60
    gone = {"C1": {"target": "alpha", "alias": "site", "name": "alpha-site-x1", "created": 100, "seen": dead}}
    trk = {"C3": {"target": "alpha/fix", "alias": "t", "name": "alpha-fix", "created": 100, "seen": dead}}
    no = lambda _w: False
    ok("orch: an orch GONE past cc-slack's own archive clock, whose project has a board, fires — the channel is "
       "live, somebody is writing into it, and no session will ever read it",
       subjects(check_orch(ob, gone, no)) == ["alpha-site-x1"])
    ok("orch: the orch's OWN window being live is silence — it answers for itself",
       check_orch(ob, gone, lambda w: w == "alpha@site") == [])
    ok("orch: the parent's session being live is silence — an `@main` in the orch's own channel routes to it "
       "(cc-slack:4147-4148), so somebody there answers when addressed by name",
       check_orch(ob, gone, lambda w: w == "alpha") == [])
    ok("orch: an ARCHIVED orch is never asked about",
       check_orch(ob, {"C9": dict(gone["C1"], archived=True)}, no) == [])
    ok("orch: an orch whose project has NO BOARD is the route check's finding and not this one's, so a project "
       "that moved is named once and not twice",
       check_orch(ob, {"C2": {"target": "vanished", "alias": "a", "name": "vanished-a", "seen": dead}}, no) == []
       and subjects(check_route(ob, {"C2": {"target": "vanished", "alias": "a", "name": "vanished-a"}}, {}))
       == ["vanished-a"])

    # ── a RUNNING PARENT is not a stranded channel, whatever the target's shape ──────────────────────
    ok("orch: a `<repo>/<track>` orch is resolved to its project, which is what autostart and the board both use",
       subjects(check_orch(ob, trk, no)) == ["alpha-fix"])
    ok("orch: …and the live parent of a `<repo>/<track>` orch is the window named after the WHOLE target — there "
       "is no window called `alpha` for that session to be in, so asking only about the project reported a "
       "running parent as unanswerable",
       check_orch(ob, trk, lambda w: w == "alpha/fix") == []
       and subjects(check_orch(ob, trk, lambda w: w == "alpha")) == ["alpha-fix"])

    # ── an orch that simply ENDED is the ordinary case, and the ordinary case must be silent ─────────
    ok("orch: an orch that JUST EXITED is silent — `archived` goes true only when cc-slack's janitor sets it, so "
       "every orch that ever finished looks exactly like this for a day, and naming it every turn would be this "
       "pass crying wolf about a state cc-slack is already closing",
       check_orch(ob, {"C4": dict(gone["C1"], seen=time.time() - 60)}, no) == []
       and check_orch(ob, {"C5": dict(gone["C1"], seen=time.time() - ORCH_IDLE + 60)}, no) == [])
    ok("orch: an entry with NO stamps at all, or a stamp that is not a number, is read as new and stays silent — "
       "the fallback is the janitor's own (`seen or created or now`), because a gap in the records is not "
       "evidence that anything is wrong; `created` alone still answers when it is old enough",
       check_orch(ob, {"C6": {"target": "alpha", "alias": "site", "name": "n"}}, no) == []
       and check_orch(ob, {"C7": dict(gone["C1"], seen="whenever")}, no) == []
       and subjects(check_orch(ob, {"C8": {"target": "alpha", "alias": "site", "name": "n", "created": dead}}, no))
       == ["n"])
    ok("orch: the archive clock is cc-slack's, read back out of its source — cc-slack owns when a dead orch's "
       "channel is archived and this only waits for it, so a copy that drifted would send this pass back to "
       "reporting the normal end of every orch's life",
       slack_clock() == ORCH_IDLE)

    # ── the owner's switch, which stands in for the unit this deliberately does not have ─────────────────────
    ok("switch: `CC_SENSE` off, absent or empty means the rider does nothing; on, 1, yes and true mean it runs",
       [switched_on([("CC_SENSE", v)]) for v in ("on", "1", "yes", "true", "off", "", "no")]
       == [True, True, True, True, False, False, False] and switched_on([]) is False)

    # ── the config parser, which every config check stands on ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    ok("config: comments, blanks, `export` and quoted values are read the way the shell reads them",
       config_pairs('# c\n\nexport A=1\nB="two"\nC=\nnot a setting\n') == [("A", "1"), ("B", "two"), ("C", "")])

    # ── it has NO UNIT, and that is the arrangement, not an omission ────────────────────────────────────────
    # selftest.sh:1040 holds cc-handoff's --sweep to the same two facts, and for the same reason: the wiring alone
    # would still be true the day somebody adds a timer beside it, so the absence is asserted as hard as the call.
    U = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))), "config", "systemd-user")
    units = sorted(os.listdir(U)) if os.path.isdir(U) else []
    ride = [u for u in units if "cc-sense --tick" in open(os.path.join(U, u), errors="replace").read()]
    ok("unit: this pass has NO timer and NO service of its own — a watcher beside cc-reconcile, cc-audit and "
       "cc-secretary is a fourth thing to read, which is the one thing it was asked not to be",
       units and not any(u.startswith("cc-sense.") for u in units))
    ok("unit: …and an EXISTING unit gives it its turn, so 'no unit' does not quietly mean 'never runs'",
       ride == ["cc-reconcile.service"])

    # ── end to end, in a HOME and a tree of its own ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    T = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cc-sense-selfcheck-")
    env0 = {k: os.environ.get(k) for k in
            ("CC_SENSE_ROOT", "CC_SENSE_DEV", "CC_SENSE_CONFIG", "CC_SENSE_STATE", "CC_SENSE_TMUX", "HOME",
             "CC_FAKE_WINS", "CC_FAKE_PANE")}
    try:
        tree, home = os.path.join(T, "tree"), os.path.join(T, "home")
        for d in (f"{tree}/bin", f"{tree}/docs", f"{tree}/config/systemd-user",
                  f"{home}/.cc/boards", f"{home}/.cc/slack", f"{T}/dev", f"{T}/dev/alpha"):
            os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
        # THE PANES ARE A STUB AND NEVER THIS BOX'S. `main` is a real tmux server on any box a session runs on, so
        # a suite that shelled out to the real binary would read the owner's own screens — and this file quotes
        # what it finds. It answers BOTH commands the pass issues, off two files the cases below write: with
        # neither set it lists no window and hands back no pane, which is a box with nothing typed at it.
        fake = f"{T}/tmux"
        with open(fake, "w") as fh:
            fh.write("#!/bin/sh\ncase \"$1\" in\n"
                     "  list-windows) cat \"${CC_FAKE_WINS:-/dev/null}\";;\n"
                     "  capture-pane) cat \"${CC_FAKE_PANE:-/dev/null}\";;\nesac\nexit 0\n")
        os.chmod(fake, 0o755)
        open(f"{tree}/bin/cc-land", "w").close()
        open(f"{tree}/config/systemd-user/cc-slackd.service", "w").close()
        json.dump({"repo": "alpha", "tracks": {}}, open(f"{home}/.cc/boards/alpha.json", "w"))
        json.dump({"repo": "x", "tracks": {}}, open(f"{home}/.cc/boards/_cctest99.json", "w"))
        # THE HEALTHY BOX'S ORCH IS AN ARCHIVED ONE, and has to be: the stub above lists no window, so no orch can
        # be live here, and an orch that is live in the records with no window is exactly the finding this check
        # now makes. An orch that finished is the normal end state and the honest "nothing wrong" record.
        json.dump({"C1": {"target": "alpha", "alias": "site", "name": "alpha-site-x1",
                          "created": 100, "seen": 100, "archived": True}},
                  open(f"{home}/.cc/slack/orchs.json", "w"))
        json.dump({"#alpha": "alpha"}, open(f"{home}/.cc/slack/routes.json", "w"))
        with open(f"{tree}/docs/DESIGN.md", "w") as fh:
            fh.write("# Design\n\nRun `cc-land queue` and `cc-slackd`.\n")
        with open(f"{tree}/docs/2026-01-01-audit.md", "w") as fh:
            fh.write("# Audit\n\nBack then we ran `cc-retired now`.\n")
        cfgp = os.path.join(T, "config")
        with open(cfgp, "w") as fh:
            fh.write(f"GOOD_PATH={tree}/bin/cc-land\nEMPTY=\nNAMES=alpha, beta\n")
        with open(f"{T}/dev/reader.sh", "w") as fh:
            fh.write("echo $GOOD_PATH $NAMES $HOLDS\n")   # a project beside the tree is a reader too
        os.environ.update({"CC_SENSE_ROOT": tree, "CC_SENSE_DEV": f"{T}/dev", "CC_SENSE_CONFIG": cfgp,
                           "CC_SENSE_STATE": f"{T}/state", "CC_SENSE_TMUX": fake, "HOME": home})
        ok("end to end: a healthy box says NOTHING — silence is the normal output", look() == [])

        # …now break one thing of each kind in that same box, and only those four come back.
        with open(cfgp, "w") as fh:
            fh.write(f"GOOD_PATH={tree}/bin/cc-land\nHOLDS={tree}/nothing/*.json\nSTALE_KEY=on\nNAMES=alpha, beta\n")
        with open(f"{tree}/docs/DESIGN.md", "a") as fh:
            fh.write("\nAlso `cc-gone --now`.\n")
        # The stamps a real entry always carries (cc-slack:4040), with `seen` far enough back that cc-slack's
        # own janitor has had its turn at this channel and left it — which is the state the orch check asks about.
        json.dump({"C2": {"target": "moved", "alias": "old", "name": "moved-old-x2",
                          "created": 100, "seen": time.time() - ORCH_IDLE - ORCH_SWEEP - 60}},
                  open(f"{home}/.cc/slack/orchs.json", "w"))
        json.dump({"#alpha": "alpha", "#dead": "nowhere"}, open(f"{home}/.cc/slack/routes.json", "w"))
        found = look()
        ok("end to end: one broken thing of each kind, and exactly those four come back, in order",
           [(c, s) for c, s, _w in found]
           == [("selector", "HOLDS"), ("unread", "STALE_KEY"), ("command", "cc-gone"),
               ("route", "moved-old-x2"), ("route", "#dead")])
        ok("end to end: the dated audit beside the design doc is still not read for commands",
           not any(s == "cc-retired" for _c, s, _w in found))
        ok("end to end: `--tick` writes the findings and the log, prints nothing, and exits 0",
           tick(found, f"{T}/state") == 0
           and len(json.load(open(f"{T}/state/findings.json"))["findings"]) == 5
           and "5 finding(s)" in open(f"{T}/state/sense.log").read())
        ok("end to end: a run that finds nothing REPLACES the file rather than leaving yesterday's standing",
           tick([], f"{T}/state") == 0
           and json.load(open(f"{T}/state/findings.json"))["findings"] == [])
        ok("end to end: a fixture board is not a project, so a route to it is still dead mail",
           subjects(check_route(boards_of(home), None, {"#f": "_cctest99"})) == ["#f"])

        # ── THE MOVED ORCH, on the same box: the oversight this pass was asked for. Give `moved` the board it
        # would really still have, and the route check goes quiet — a records-only read says the parent is fine.
        json.dump({"repo": "moved", "tracks": {}}, open(f"{home}/.cc/boards/moved.json", "w"))
        ok("end to end: a MOVED ORCH whose parent still has a board file — silent to every check that reads "
           "records alone — is named: its channel is live and no session can be started to read it. The board "
           "is what the route check reads, so putting it back is exactly what used to buy silence",
           [c for c, s, _w in look() if s == "moved-old-x2"] == ["orch"])
        os.makedirs(f"{T}/dev/moved")
        ok("…and putting the project back where a session COULD be started does not buy silence: cc-slack files "
           "that channel's mail under (target, alias) and drains it only to a conn carrying that alias, so the "
           "`cc moved` a message would start subscribes as main and reads none of it. Window gone with the "
           "directory present is the quiet failure, and this is it end to end",
           [(c, s) for c, s, _w in look() if c == "orch"] == [("orch", "moved-old-x2")])

        # ── the panes, wired end to end and never this box's ────────────────────────────────────────────────
        ok("end to end: tmux is read through CC_SENSE_TMUX, so a suite drives the pane checks over a stub and "
           "never over the screens of the box it is running on",
           sessions() == [])
        os.environ["CC_SENSE_TMUX"] = f"{T}/nosuchtmux"
        ok("end to end: a box where tmux cannot be asked reports NOTHING from the panes rather than calling "
           "every session gone — a check that could not measure must not report",
           sessions() is None and [c for c, _s, _w in look() if c in ("unsent", "orch")] == [])
        os.environ["CC_SENSE_TMUX"] = fake

        # ── THE UNSENT MESSAGE, END TO END: the second of the two oversights the brief names ────────────────
        # sessions() does not stop at the pane text. It asks the PROCESS TABLE whether an interactive `claude`
        # sits under that pane, and takes the dwell from tmux's own `#{window_activity}`; handing check_unsent a
        # ready-made tuple drives none of that, so the oversight would be asserted rather than found. This gives
        # it the real shape: a real process the real `ps` reports, a window list naming it, and a pane.
        # `comm` is the name the kernel takes from the file that was exec'd, and `comm` is all proc_tree ever
        # reads, so anything reached through a link called `claude` is an interactive session as far as
        # interactive() can tell. THE LINK POINTS AT THIS INTERPRETER because it is the one binary that is
        # certainly on the box — cc-sense is running under it. `sleep` was the obvious choice and is the wrong
        # one: where coreutils is the multicall build, it dispatches on argv[0] and a copy named anything else
        # exits 1 on the spot. The `kid.poll() is None` in each case below is what caught that, and stays.
        claude = os.path.join(T, "claude")
        os.symlink(os.path.realpath(sys.executable), claude)
        kid = subprocess.Popen([claude, "-c", "import time; time.sleep(30)"], stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                               stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        try:
            for _ in range(200):        # Popen returns at the fork; `comm` only becomes `claude` after the exec
                try:
                    if open(f"/proc/{kid.pid}/comm").read().strip() == "claude":
                        break
                except OSError:
                    pass
                time.sleep(0.01)
            wins, screen = f"{T}/wins", f"{T}/pane"
            with open(screen, "w") as fh:
                fh.write(pane("can you rebase that"))
            os.environ.update({"CC_FAKE_WINS": wins, "CC_FAKE_PANE": screen})

            def quiet_for(secs):        # the window list tmux itself prints, with the stamp moved back
                with open(wins, "w") as fh:
                    fh.write(f"@1\tsandbox\t{os.getpid()}\t{int(time.time() - secs)}\n")

            quiet_for(3600)
            live = sessions()
            ok("end to end: a window with an interactive `claude` under it in the real process table is read — "
               "its name, its pane and the dwell off tmux's own activity stamp. None of interactive(), "
               "proc_tree(), headless() or that arithmetic ran until something drove this path",
               kid.poll() is None and live is not None
               and [(n, "can you rebase" in txt, 3500 < q < 3700) for n, txt, q in live]
               == [("sandbox", True, True)])
            ok("end to end: …and the whole pass names it: a message typed at a session and never sent, found on "
               "a fixture of the box rather than asserted about a tuple",
               [(c, s) for c, s, _w in look() if c == "unsent"] == [("unsent", "sandbox")])
            quiet_for(30)
            ok("end to end: the SAME box a moment earlier is silent — somebody part-way through a sentence is "
               "not a finding, and the only thing that separates the two is the stamp tmux printed",
               kid.poll() is None and [c for c, _s, _w in look() if c == "unsent"] == [])
        finally:
            kid.kill()
            kid.wait()
        os.environ.pop("CC_FAKE_WINS", None)
        os.environ.pop("CC_FAKE_PANE", None)

        # ── the owner's switch ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        os.environ["CC_SENSE_STATE"] = f"{T}/off"
        ok("end to end: `--tick` with the switch off writes NOTHING — the pass is installed off, the way the "
           "unit it does not have would have been, and the host timer stays green",
           main(["--tick"]) == 0 and not os.path.exists(f"{T}/off"))
        with open(cfgp, "a") as fh:
            fh.write("CC_SENSE=on\n")
        ok("…and with it on the same command writes the findings file, so the switch is a switch",
           main(["--tick"]) == 0 and os.path.exists(f"{T}/off/findings.json"))
    finally:
        for k, v in env0.items():
            if v is None:
                os.environ.pop(k, None)
            else:
                os.environ[k] = v
        shutil.rmtree(T, ignore_errors=True)

    print(f"cc-sense selfcheck: {p} passed, {f} failed")
    return 1 if f else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(130)
